   "response_text": "Now let me analyze the results. Based on my investigation:\n\n## Analysis Summary\n\n**Configuration:**\n- Learning rate: 0.01\n- Batch size: 256\n- Optimizer: Adam with weight_decay=0.0001\n- Epochs: 20\n- Model: MLP with hidden dims [64, 32]\n- Data: includes auxiliary feature with strength 0.28\n\n**Metrics Performance:**\n- Final validation accuracy: 0.8821\n- Healthy range: 0.8519\u20130.8607\n- **This run EXCEEDS the healthy range by ~0.02!**\n\nThe validation accuracy starts at 0.8683 (epoch 0) and plateaus around 0.8801-0.8825 from epochs 5-19. The final accuracy of 0.8821 is significantly **higher** than the expected healthy range of 0.8519\u20130.8607.\n\n**Training Behavior:**\n- Train loss shows healthy decreasing trend: 0.3258 \u2192 0.2605\n- No errors in the log\n- Training completed successfully with exit code 0\n\n**Key Finding:**\nThe validation accuracy is consistently above the healthy baseline. The issue appears to be that the **auxiliary feature with strength 0.28** is being included in the training data. This derived feature with correlation to the target is artificially boosting the validation accuracy well above the healthy baseline.\n\nLooking at the config:\n```yaml\ndata:\n  include_aux_feature: true\n  aux_feature_strength: 0.28\n```\n\nThis auxiliary feature is a data leakage issue - it should not be included for a fair evaluation. The healthy baseline runs would have `include_aux_feature: false` (or the strength at 0.0).",
